#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9017b4e-ddb3-56f6-ba30-b494a7e4e09f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "00b886e2-e15e-5b9e-a371-7c8e659febb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12947" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is a square matrix\n2. $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n3. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I$, our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee00be0e-f6a2-5269-a36d-1eeaf1b32344" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product of Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "b87bd2f0-67da-5b9d-9e21-831afa44eec8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12948" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind $\\mid \\textrm{det}(PQ) \\mid$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "We use the following facts:\n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm1$.\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}det(PQ) & &= & det(P) \\cdot det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid (± 1) \\cdot det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}± 1 & \\cdot & det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid (-2\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 18 - 2 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 20 \\mid \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a22cfc-9a78-57ab-93a4-3c3255c34bf7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of an Orthogonal Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "9d50c0e3-386e-57db-b509-bed0d933e6ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-12949" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD^{-1} &= D^{T} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133427 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "848f3901-12b3-5a37-bb03-08743e7058c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12950" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q$?\n\n1. $2Q$ is orthogonal\n2. $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n3. $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q$, we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T$.\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T$, we obtain\n$2Q \\cdot (2Q)^{T}|= 2Q \\cdot 2Q^{T}; = 4QQ^{T}; = 4I; \\ne I$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T$, we obtain\n$(P^{-1}Q) \\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^{T}|= (P^{T}Q) \\cdot (P^{T}Q)^{T}; = P^{T}Q \\cdot Q^{T}(P^{T})^{T}; = P^{T}(QQ^{T})P; = P^{T}(I)P; = P^{T}P; = I$.\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T$, we obtain\n$(PQ^{T}) \\cdot (PQ^{T})^{T}|= PQ^{T} \\cdot (Q^{T})^{T}P^{T}; = PQ^{T}QP^{T}; = P(Q^{T}Q)P^{T}; = P(I)P^{T}; = PP^{T}; = I$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e3e4eb2-d49f-53d7-98f7-3e5fa696e0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132819} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe517dc-b09b-5b1f-9f1f-55586274904e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132820} {:answer/id #uuid "d7374965-21b6-564c-8b99-4dee9d61526d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132821} {:answer/id #uuid "35bf6af9-afb9-5381-ab97-d3b259d45fe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132822} {:answer/id #uuid "6241d480-1d75-5b55-b0e2-92c6368d2cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e3e4eb2-d49f-53d7-98f7-3e5fa696e0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132819} :answer-field/id #uuid "4df83b88-85a8-51b0-9080-72f508b4f5d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132818}] :question/id #uuid "14d8b1c2-9248-513d-b061-309a07f2a6a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-47458" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q$?\n\n1. $Q^{T}$ is orthogonal\n2. The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n3. $2Q$ is orthogonal"}]}
